# 100. Same Tree

## Problem

Given the roots of two binary trees `p` and `q`, write a function to check if they are the same or not.

Two binary trees are considered the same if they are structurally identical, and the nodes have the same value.

## Example

Input: `p = [1,2,3], q = [1,2,3]`

Output: `true`

## Solution

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (p == null && q == null) return true;
        if (p == null || q == null) return false;
        if (p.val != q.val) return false;
        return isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}
```

## Explanation

The `isSameTree` function takes two binary trees `p` and `q` as input and returns a boolean value indicating whether they are the same or not.

The function first checks if both `p` and `q` are null. If they are, it means both trees are empty and are considered the same, so it returns `true`.

Next, it checks if either `p` or `q` is null. If one of them is null and the other is not, it means the trees are not the same, so it returns `false`.

If both `p` and `q` are not null, it compares the values of their root nodes. If the values are not equal, it means the trees are not the same, so it returns `false`.

If the values are equal, it recursively calls the `isSameTree` function on the left subtrees of `p` and `q` and on the right subtrees of `p` and `q`. It returns `true` only if both recursive calls return `true`, indicating that the left and right subtrees are also the same.

By following this approach, the function checks if the two binary trees are structurally identical and have the same values at each node. If they are, it returns `true`; otherwise, it returns `false`.

