## 1. [100. Same Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/same-tree/)

### Solution

```python
# Definition for a binary tree node.
# class TreeNode:
#     def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):
#         self.val = val
#         self.left = left
#         self.right = right
class Solution:
    def isSameTree(self, p: Optional[TreeNode], q: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        if not p and not q:
            return True
        if not p or not q:
            return False
        if p.val != q.val:
            return False
        return self.isSameTree(p.left, q.left) and self.isSameTree(p.right, q.right)
```

### Explanation

This solution uses a recursive approach to compare the two trees. It checks if both trees are empty, in which case they are considered the same. If one tree is empty and the other is not, they are not the same. If the values of the current nodes are different, they are not the same. Finally, it recursively compares the left and right subtrees of both trees.

### Time and Space Complexity

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the smaller tree. The space complexity is O(h), where h is the height of the smaller tree, due to the recursive call stack.

## 2. [101. Symmetric Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/symmetric-tree/)

### Solution

```python
# Definition for a binary tree node.
# class TreeNode:
#     def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):
#         self.val = val
#         self.left = left
#         self.right = right
class Solution:
    def isSymmetric(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        if not root:
            return True
        return self.isMirror(root.left, root.right)

    def isMirror(self, left: Optional[TreeNode], right: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        if not left and not right:
            return True
        if not left or not right:
            return False
        if left.val != right.val:
            return False
        return self.isMirror(left.left, right.right) and self.isMirror(left.right, right.left)
```

### Explanation

This solution uses a recursive approach to check if the tree is symmetric. It first checks if the root is empty, in which case it is considered symmetric. Then, it calls the `isMirror` function to check if the left and right subtrees are mirrors of each other. The `isMirror` function checks if both subtrees are empty, in which case they are considered mirrors. If one subtree is empty and the other is not, they are not mirrors. If the values of the current nodes are different, they are not mirrors. Finally, it recursively checks if the left subtree of the left subtree is a mirror of the right subtree of the right subtree, and if the right subtree of the left subtree is a mirror of the left subtree of the right subtree.

### Time and Space Complexity

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the tree. The space complexity is O(h), where h is the height of the tree, due to the recursive call stack.

## 3. [104. Maximum Depth of Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-depth-of-binary-tree/)

