## Solution

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (p == null && q == null) {
            return true;
        }
        if (p == null || q == null) {
            return false;
        }
        if (p.val != q.val) {
            return false;
        }
        return isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}
```

## Explanation

The solution uses a recursive approach to compare the two trees. It checks if both trees are null, in which case they are considered the same. If one of the trees is null and the other is not, they are not the same. If the values of the current nodes are not equal, they are not the same. Finally, it recursively compares the left and right subtrees of both trees. If both subtrees are the same, the trees are considered the same. Otherwise, they are not the same.

## Time Complexity

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the larger of the two trees. This is because the solution visits each node once.

## Space Complexity

The space complexity of this solution is O(h), where h is the height of the larger of the two trees. This is because the solution uses recursion, and the maximum depth of the recursion stack is equal to the height of the tree. In the worst case, the height of the tree is equal to the number of nodes, resulting in a space complexity of O(n).
